WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.200
"Hepatitis E Virus in Pork"

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:08.120
You've probably heard
of hepatitis A,

00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:09.760
which you can get from
eating unsanitary food

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:11.160
in the developing world.

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:12.680
Then, we discovered hepatitis B,

00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:15.240
which is spread by infected
blood and bodily fluids.

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:18.320
And then, hepatitis C,
mostly from IV drug use.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:20.080
These days
we're up to hepatitis E,

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:24.160
which is actually the main cause
of viral hepatitis worldwide.

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:26.120
It was first discovered
in the early 80s,

00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:28.200
but we didn't have a clue
where it was coming from

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:29.720
until 1997,

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:31.800
when it was discovered
in more than 90 percent

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:34.440
of tested U.S. pig farms.

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:35.880
From the
viral hepatitis division

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:37.880
of the Centers for
Disease Control and Prevention,

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:41.160
the CDC:
"Much meat; much malady."

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:43.920
Hepatitis E
is now considered a zoonosis,

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:46.200
an animal-to-human disease

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:49.000
that causes 20 million
human cases a year,

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:50.120
primarily from pigs

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:53.320
via the consumption
of infected pork products.

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:55.680
The hepatitis E virus
has since been detected

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:57.400
on pig farms worldwide,

00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:01.200
including 80 to 100 percent
of U.S. operations,

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:04.520
where it's spread
via the fecal-oral route.

00:01:04.520 --> 00:01:06.960
So, no surprise there's such
widespread infection

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:08.960
on industrial
confinement operations

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:11.880
given how closely
packed together the animals are.

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:14.120
Though if you test the pigs
where it counts,

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:16.840
in terms of food safety
at the slaughterhouse,

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:19.680
only six percent of U.S. pigs
have active infections,

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:21.040
meaning infectious virus

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:22.200
flowing through
their bloodstreams

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:24.000
at the time of slaughter.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:25.360
At a retail level,

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:28.520
about 10 percent of commercial
pork products tested worldwide

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:30.360
contain the hepatitis E virus,

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:32.520
found in pork meat, and sausage,

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:34.760
as well as organ meats
like the liver,

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:37.240
with the highest rates
found in North America.

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:38.920
So, for example, in California,

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:40.600
though only
12 percent of samples

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:42.200
were positive for the virus,

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:45.440
most of the retail packages had
at least one positive sample,

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:49.040
so most were infected
with hepatitis E.

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:50.600
Perhaps this helps explain

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:52.440
why there's
an even tighter correlation

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:55.120
between pork consumption
and liver cirrhosis

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:56.120
than there is between

00:01:56.120 --> 00:01:58.280
alcohol consumption
and liver cirrhosis

00:01:58.280 --> 00:02:00.240
on a country-by-country basis.

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:03.440
But just because there's
infectious virus in retail meat,

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:06.120
does that mean
it could survive cooking?

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:08.000
Unfortunately,
it seems that some virus

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:10.240
would most likely survive
the internal temperatures

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:12.320
of rare-cooked meat.

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:14.880
So, how can we avoid infection?

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:18.320
According to the former chief of
the CDC's viral hepatitis lab,

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:20.480
the most expedient way
to prevent meats,

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:22.280
organ meats, or seafood

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:23.920
from conveying hepatitis

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:25.960
is to avoid eating them,

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:28.760
or in the very least ensuring
that they are thoroughly cooked

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:29.840
before being consumed.

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:32.320
Unfortunately,
in addition to the potential

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:36.360
for the hepatitis E virus to
survive some cooking processes,

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:38.560
raw blood products
are commonly used

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:41.200
in ready-cooked foods like ham.

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:43.600
Porcine blood--pig's blood--

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:45.800
used as an ingredient
in meat products

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:49.760
may serve as a vehicle
for hepatitis E transmission.

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:52.560
Blood components are ingredients
used by the meat industry

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:55.280
as color enhancers
or emulsifiers,

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:56.520
and surprisingly,

00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:59.120
none of these blood products
appears to be heat treated

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:01.280
prior to being processed
into food.

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:02.240
The virus was found

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:04.440
in 90 percent
of liquid blood products,

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:07.520
and 30 percent
of powdered blood.

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:09.760
That helps explain why
the incidence of hepatitis E

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:11.400
is significantly lower

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:13.560
among blood donors
who don't eat meat,

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:16.240
indicating that meat consumption
is a major risk factor

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:17.800
for hepatitis E infection,

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:20.000
with those eating pork products
like ham or bacon

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:22.400
at about triple the odds
of infection.

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:23.960
Though some studies found

00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:26.440
no cases of infection
among vegetarians,

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:29.360
others just showed a
significantly lower prevalence.

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:33.520
How might vegetarians
be getting hepatitis E?

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:35.080
It could be
through dairy products.

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:37.280
Cows can become infected
with the virus too,

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:39.800
and their milk contaminated
with infectious hepatitis E

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:42.200
is now recognized
as a new potential

00:03:42.200 --> 00:03:44.640
for high risk of transmission
to humans.

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:46.900
Even when infected milk
has been fully pasteurized,

00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:49.440
it was still found
to be infectious.

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:51.080
But, the most likely cause

00:03:51.080 --> 00:03:53.120
of hepatitis E infection
in vegetarians

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:55.560
may be other people.

00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:58.280
Once we're infected
through our dietary habits,

00:03:58.280 --> 00:04:00.200
we can transmit it to others

00:04:00.200 --> 00:04:03.920
via not washing our hands
after using the bathroom,

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:06.000
or potentially in the bedroom,

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:09.568
though this has not yet
been confirmed.

